# Visual handoff

For any change that would affect a major UX, visual, or animation part of the game, stop and ask whether it needs a dedicated sprite, asset, or visual element that should be drawn elsewhere.

## Do

1. Think about it on purpose. Engine modulate, a Label, a recolor, or a reused mount is fine when that is actually enough.
2. If a new drawing would make the screen read clearly — or if you are stretching, hiding, or faking something the player will stare at — **tell Nathan in that turn**.
3. Package **one** prompt or file he can paste to the visual-designer agent. That packet must be enough for them to return files this repo can drop in without a follow-up interview.

## The packet must name

- Exact PNG filenames and folder (`assets/dungeon/...`)
- Native pixel size of each file, then 4× nearest-neighbor (same pipeline as the rest of the game)
- Transparent vs opaque, registration / pivot, and which frames loop
- Palette hexes and what **not** to draw (no English on plaques, no redrawing card art, unless the request says otherwise)
- How code will use it (overlay, additive glint, empty 16×16 well, nine-slice, and so on)
- A one-page size list they return with the PNGs

## Don't

- Ship a stretched, illegible, or placeholder stand-in and call the visual done when a real asset is what would fix it.
- Ask Nathan to write the designer prompt.
- Scatter the request across chat when a single file would do.

Feel, layout, and copy can still be built the same turn with what is already in the folder. The handoff is for the missing drawing, not a reason to skip the rest of the request.
